"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][29]={"university_physics_en:M29-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M29-L1 – Equivalence principle and local inertial frames<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M29 – Master: General relativity and cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M10: special relativity; M26:\ngeometry; M28: dynamical models · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish locally removable\nfree-fall acceleration from residual tidal effects.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Why does a person in free fall feel weightless for a short interval\nwhile moving through a gravitational field? The equivalence principle\nstates that physics in a sufficiently small freely falling laboratory\nagrees locally with physics in an inertial frame without gravity. An\nobserver held at rest on the ground instead has a nonzero proper\nacceleration measured by an accelerometer.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Near Earth’s surface a small laboratory can approximate the field as\nuniform with strength\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTwo identical bodies initially at rest relative to the ground acquire\nspeed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">gt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen drag is neglected. In a freely falling laboratory their relative\nacceleration is approximately zero. Comparing an elevator held at rest\nin gravity to a rocket accelerating upward at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis only a <strong>local<\/strong> equivalence: the strength and direction\nof a real field vary across extended regions. These differences cause\nmeasurable relative accelerations and cannot be transformed away\neverywhere. For a small height difference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin a static weak field, clock rates also differ by approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g\\Delta z/c^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>f<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>≃<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≪<\/mo><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">weak static field<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\Delta f}{f}\\simeq\\frac{g\\,\\Delta z}{c^2}\\qquad (|g\\Delta z|\\ll c^2,\\ \\text{weak static field}).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta z=10\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=9.81\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.998<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c\\approx2.998\\times10^8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.09<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>15<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta f/f\\approx1.09\\times10^{-15}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe stationary clock at higher altitude runs slightly faster in this\ncomparison. The drawing contrasts the local elevator/rocket analogy with\na nonlocal setting distinguishable through field gradients.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_084_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two labeled sketches compare uniform acceleration in a small cabin with radial field lines diverging across a larger region.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two labeled sketches compare uniform\nacceleration in a small cabin with radial field lines diverging across a\nlarger region.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Acceleration can mimic uniform gravity locally; tidal effects\nmark the limit of the analogy.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which of a freely falling clock and a clock supported on the ground\nhas nonzero local proper acceleration?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta f/f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles in the approximation?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can one coordinate choice not remove Earth’s gravitational\neffects everywhere?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The supported clock (and its accelerometer); the freely falling\nclock has approximately zero proper acceleration.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It doubles to about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.18<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>15<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.18\\times10^{-15}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Tidal effects from spatially varying gravity remain.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The calculation assumes small height separations, a static weak\nfield, and stationary observers. The equivalence principle does not\nassert that gravity and acceleration are globally identical; tidal\neffects remain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Local freedom from gravitational acceleration leads to a\nposition-dependent spacetime metric. M29-L2 formalizes proper time and\nlight cones. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M29&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/general-relativity/grhtml/S1\">Tong – Geodesics in Spacetime<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §§1.1–1.2; equivalence principle, time dilation, and weak field; Subject relevance to lesson: M29-L1 – Equivalence principle and local inertial frames · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M29-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M29-L2 – Metric, proper time, and light cones<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M29 – Master: General relativity and cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M10: Minkowski space; M26: tensors\nand coordinates; M29-L1: equivalence principle · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M29&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compute proper time along\ntimelike paths from the metric line element and identify null\ndirections.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A coordinate is not itself a clock reading. In relativity, a clock\ncarried along a worldline measures proper time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe spacetime metric determines invariant lengths and intervals. Light\ncones distinguish possible causal signals from spacelike-separated\nevents.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>We adopt signature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-,+,+,+)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^0=ct<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe general line element is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msup><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ds^2=g_{\\mu\\nu}dx^\\mu dx^\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a timelike path,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>τ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ds^2=-c^2d\\tau^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor light in geometric optics,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ds^2=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a local Minkowski chart,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ds^2=-c^2dt^2+dx^2+dy^2+dz^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSubstituting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^2=(dx^2+dy^2+dz^2)/dt^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the usual time dilation law. In curved spacetime, the constant\nMinkowski coefficients are replaced by position-dependent metric\ncomponents.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(in an inertial Minkowski chart)<\/mtext><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\tau=dt\\sqrt{1-\\frac{v^2}{c^2}}\\qquad\\text{(in an inertial Minkowski chart)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>A clock travels at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0.6c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor coordinate time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=5\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin an inertial frame. Its elapsed proper time is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.36<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\tau=5\\sqrt{1-0.36}=4\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ct<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>–<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndiagram, the lines\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\pm ct<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare null directions. The particle worldline remains inside the future\nlight cone.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_174_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Spacetime diagram with horizontal position and vertical time axes, a light cone, and a timelike worldline inside the cone.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Spacetime diagram with horizontal\nposition and vertical time axes, a light cone, and a timelike worldline\ninside the cone.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Null lines bound the local causal region; massive particles\nfollow timelike worldlines.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What condition defines a null line element?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What proper time elapses in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof coordinate time at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0.8c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ds^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninvariant although individual\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_{\\mu\\nu}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndepend on coordinates?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ds^2=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\tau=6\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Metric components and coordinate differentials transform together,\nleaving their contraction unchanged.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The\nconstant-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nformula assumes an inertial chart in flat spacetime. In curved spacetime\nor during acceleration, proper time must be integrated along the actual\nworldline. A single light-cone diagram does not determine global causal\nstructure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The metric fixes clocks, intervals, and causal directions. Next,\ncovariant derivatives describe force-free motion. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M29&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/general-relativity/grhtml/S1\">Tong – Geodesics in Spacetime<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §1: metric, proper time, and geodesics; Subject relevance to lesson: M29-L2 – Metric, proper time, and light cones · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M29-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M29-L3 – Covariant derivative and the geodesic equation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M29 – Master: General relativity and cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M26: vector and tensor calculus;\nM29-L2: metric and proper time · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M29&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain the geodesic equation\nthrough parallel transport and interpret connection coefficients in\npolar coordinates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Even in flat geometry, a straight trajectory expressed in curvilinear\ncoordinates can have nonzero coordinate acceleration. Christoffel\nsymbols describe how the coordinate basis changes. Their presence alone\ndoes not establish physical curvature.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>The Levi-Civita connection is metric-compatible and torsion-free. Its\ncoefficients are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\beta}=(1/2)g^{\\mu\\nu}(\\partial_\\alpha g_{\\nu\\beta}+\\partial_\\beta g_{\\nu\\alpha}-\\partial_\\nu g_{\\alpha\\beta})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAlong an affinely parametrized geodesic, the tangent vector transports\nparallel to itself. In the Euclidean plane with polar metric\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>φ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ds^2=dr^2+r^2d\\varphi^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>r<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma^r_{\\varphi\\varphi}=-r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma^\\varphi_{r\\varphi}=1/r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe radial equation therefore contains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><msup><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-r\\dot\\varphi^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis does not imply intrinsic curvature of the plane.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msubsup><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{d^2x^\\mu}{d\\lambda^2}+\\Gamma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\beta}\\frac{dx^\\alpha}{d\\lambda}\\frac{dx^\\beta}{d\\lambda}=0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>A point travels along a straight line of constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand varying\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the Euclidean plane. Its Cartesian accelerations vanish, while\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>r<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ddot r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ddot\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare generally nonzero in polar coordinates. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot\\varphi=0.5\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe connection term is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><msup><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-r\\dot\\varphi^2=-0.5\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe drawing places a Cartesian straight path on a polar grid.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_049_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Polar coordinate grid with concentric circles and radial rays; a straight trajectory crosses curved coordinate lines.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Polar coordinate grid with concentric\ncircles and radial rays; a straight trajectory crosses curved coordinate\nlines.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Nonzero Christoffel symbols can be an effect of coordinates\nalone.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name the two defining conditions of the Levi-Civita connection.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Evaluate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><msup><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-r\\dot\\varphi^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=4\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot\\varphi=0.25\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is the Euclidean plane curved because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>r<\/mi><\/msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma^r_{\\varphi\\varphi}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be nonzero?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Metric compatibility and zero torsion.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.25\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; the coefficients depend on coordinates but the Riemann tensor\nvanishes.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The geodesic equation describes free test bodies without\nnongravitational forces. Affine parametrization is essential for a zero\nright-hand side. Christoffel symbols are not tensors, and individual\ncoefficients are not coordinate-independent observables.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The connection explains local changes of direction. The\nnoncommutativity of covariant derivatives leads to measurable curvature\nin M29-L4. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M29&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/general-relativity/grhtml/S3\">Tong – Introducing Riemannian Geometry<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §§3.2–3.3: connection and geodesics; Subject relevance to lesson: M29-L3 – Covariant derivative and the geodesic equation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M29-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M29-L4 – Riemann curvature and geodesic deviation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M29 – Master: General relativity and cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M26: differential geometry; M29-L3:\ncovariant derivatives · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M29&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Separate coordinate-independent\ncurvature from connection coefficients and calculate curvature on a\nsphere.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>When nearby freely falling worldlines accelerate relative to one\nanother, tidal gravity is present. The mathematical object is the\nRiemann tensor. In flat spacetime it vanishes even if individual\nChristoffel symbols happen to be nonzero in a chosen chart.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>The commutator of covariant derivatives on a vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>V<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V^\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndefines curvature for a torsion-free connection by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><msup><mi>V<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mrow><\/mrow><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msup><mi>V<\/mi><mi>σ<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\nabla_\\mu,\\nabla_\\nu]V^\\rho=R^\\rho{}_{\\sigma\\mu\\nu}V^\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(this fixes our sign convention). Geodesic deviation relates the\ncurvature tensor to the relative motion of nearby freely falling\ntrajectories. An accessible two-dimensional example is the surface of a\nsphere of radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nits Gaussian curvature is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=1/R^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neverywhere. A triangle bounded by three great-circle arcs has angular\nexcess\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A/R^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nits surface area.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>α<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>A<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(sphere, great-circle triangle)<\/mtext><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=\\frac{1}{R^2},\\qquad \\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma-\\pi=KA=\\frac{A}{R^2}\\quad\\text{(sphere, great-circle triangle)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For a sphere of radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=0.25\\,\\mathrm{m^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a great-circle triangle with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=2\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe angular excess is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><msup><mn>28.65<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5\\,\\mathrm{rad}\\approx28.65^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure sketches a spherical triangle bounded by great-circle arcs,\nnot a planar unfolding.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_063_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Sphere with two schematic great-circle arcs and a highlighted spherical triangle; three angles are marked.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Sphere with two schematic great-circle\narcs and a highlighted spherical triangle; three angles are\nmarked.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The angular excess of a great-circle triangle measures positive\nspherical curvature.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a spherical surface with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=5\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the angular excess for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=1\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non a sphere with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma^\\rho_{\\mu\\nu}\\neq0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot prove physical curvature?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.04<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.04\\,\\mathrm{m^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.25\\,\\mathrm{rad}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Connection coefficients depend on coordinates; curvature is\ndescribed tensorially.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The sphere illustrates positive spatial surface curvature, not\nfour-dimensional Lorentzian spacetime directly. Spacetime geodesic\ndeviation requires timelike or null directions together with a\nconsistent sign convention.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Curvature is observable independently of coordinates. M29-L5 couples\ngeometry to matter and energy through the Einstein equations. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M29&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/general-relativity/grhtml/S3\">Tong – Introducing Riemannian Geometry<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §3.4: Riemann curvature and geometric interpretation; Subject relevance to lesson: M29-L4 – Riemann curvature and geodesic deviation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M29-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M29-L5 – Einstein equations and the Newtonian limit<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M29 – Master: General relativity and cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M29-L4: curvature; M3: Newtonian\nmechanics; M26: tensor notation · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M29&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Identify terms in the Einstein\nequations and connect the weak static limit to Poisson’s equation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A geometric account of gravity requires field equations: matter and\nenergy shape spacetime, while the metric determines how freely moving\nbodies travel. Einstein’s equations relate the Einstein tensor to\nstress–energy. A cosmological constant is allowed as an additional\ngeometric term.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>With signature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-,+,+,+)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the Riemann convention of M29-L4, write\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_{\\mu\\nu}=R_{\\mu\\nu}-(1/2)Rg_{\\mu\\nu}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor slowly moving matter,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>00<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{00}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis related to energy density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho c^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCovariant conservation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\mu T^{\\mu\\nu}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis compatible with the field equations through the Bianchi identity. In\na static weak field with negligible pressure,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mn>00<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≃<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_{00}\\simeq-(1+2\\Phi/c^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Lambda=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe suitable limit recovers Poisson’s equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla^2\\Phi=4\\pi G\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith <strong>mass density<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ρ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>R<\/mi><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/mrow><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{\\mu\\nu}-\\frac12Rg_{\\mu\\nu}+\\Lambda g_{\\mu\\nu}=\\frac{8\\pi G}{c^4}T_{\\mu\\nu}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For a uniform-density sphere with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=5500\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Newtonian interior field grows linearly:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|g(r)|=4\\pi G\\rho r/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=1000\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6.6743<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>11<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G\\approx6.6743\\times10^{-11}\\,\\mathrm{m^3/(kg\\,s^2)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit equals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.54<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.54\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graphic shows the piecewise field strength of an idealized uniform\nsphere; this is a Newtonian limit, not a full solution of Einstein’s\nequations.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_078_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Plot of the gravitational field magnitude of an ideal sphere: linear increase inside and inverse-square decrease outside.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Plot of the gravitational field magnitude\nof an ideal sphere: linear increase inside and inverse-square decrease\noutside.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The piecewise field is a consequence of the Newtonian limit for\nconstant density.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{\\mu\\nu}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresent?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What are the units of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla^2\\Phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand do they match\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\pi G\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|g(r)|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nscale with radius inside a uniform sphere?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The stress–energy tensor of matter and nongravitational fields.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{s^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the same unit.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It doubles when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles, provided both points are inside the sphere.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The Newtonian limit needs weak nearly static fields, slow matter, and\nnegligible relativistic pressure. The field equations alone do not\nspecify a solution without a source model, boundary conditions, and\ncoordinate choices.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The Einstein equations connect matter to curvature and recover\nNewtonian gravity in the appropriate regime. M29-L6 studies a spherical\nvacuum solution. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M29&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/general-relativity/grhtml/S4\">Tong – The Einstein Equations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §4: Einstein tensor and stress–energy; Subject relevance to lesson: M29-L5 – Einstein equations and the Newtonian limit · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/general-relativity/grhtml/S5\">Tong – When Gravity is Weak<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §5: weak fields and the Newtonian limit; Subject relevance to lesson: M29-L5 – Einstein equations and the Newtonian limit · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M29-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M29-L6 – Schwarzschild spacetime and gravitational redshift<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M29 – Master: General relativity and cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M29-L2: proper time; M29-L5: field\nequations; M20: stellar physics · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M29&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compute a clock rate and a\nfrequency shift between stationary observers from the static\nSchwarzschild metric.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Outside a spherical nonrotating mass, the vacuum geometry is\ndescribed by the Schwarzschild solution. A stationary clock in this\nstatic field measures different proper time per unit Schwarzschild\ncoordinate time depending on radius. Light exchanged between stationary\nemitters and receivers consequently changes frequency.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_s=2GM/c^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>θ<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>φ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\Omega^2=d\\theta^2+\\sin^2\\theta\\,d\\varphi^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOutside the source the Schwarzschild metric is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ds^2=-(1-r_s/r)c^2dt^2+(1-r_s/r)^{-1}dr^2+r^2d\\Omega^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a stationary clock at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r,\\theta,\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\tau/dt=\\sqrt{1-r_s/r}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAlong a light ray in the static geometry, the corresponding Killing\nenergy is conserved. For emission at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand reception by a stationary observer at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_o<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_o/f_e=\\sqrt{(1-r_s/r_e)/(1-r_s/r_o)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor an observer very far away, the denominator approaches one.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{f_{\\infty}}{f_e}=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{r_s}{r_e}},\\qquad r_s=\\frac{2GM}{c^2},\\quad r_e&gt;r_s.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>A stationary emitter at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_e=10r_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsends a light signal. A stationary receiver at infinity measures\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.94868<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\infty/f_e=\\sqrt{0.9}\\approx0.94868<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na decrease of about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.13<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5.13\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe plot shows this factor versus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r/r_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe curve applies only to the frequency measurements of stationary\nobservers in the exterior region.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_258_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Curve of the frequency ratio at infinity versus radius in Schwarzschild-radius units; the value at ten radii is marked.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Curve of the frequency ratio at infinity\nversus radius in Schwarzschild-radius units; the value at ten radii is\nmarked.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>A signal emitted deeper in a static gravitational field is\nmeasured at lower frequency far away.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\infty/f_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_e=4r_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the dimension of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can a stationary timelike worldline in Schwarzschild coordinates\nextend through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=r_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.8660<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{3/4}\\approx0.8660<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Length;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">GM/c^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas units of metres.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; a massive observer cannot remain stationary at the horizon.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Schwarzschild describes a spherical exterior with no rotation or\ncharge. The coordinate singularity at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not a physical curvature singularity. Real emitters and observers can\nalso have Doppler shifts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The static exterior predicts clock rates and light shifts. M29-L7\nmoves to a time-dependent cosmological geometry. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M29&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/general-relativity/grhtml/S6\">Tong – Black Holes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §6: Schwarzschild geometry and horizons; Subject relevance to lesson: M29-L6 – Schwarzschild spacetime and gravitational redshift · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/general-relativity/grhtml/S1\">Tong – Geodesics in Spacetime<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §1.3: Schwarzschild clocks and redshift; Subject relevance to lesson: M29-L6 – Schwarzschild spacetime and gravitational redshift · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M29-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M29-L7 – FLRW cosmology and Friedmann dynamics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M29 – Master: General relativity and cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M29-L5: Einstein equations; M29-L6:\nclocks and light; M20: cosmological observations · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M29&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish scale-factor\nevolution in flat matter- and radiation-dominated models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>On very large scales, the universe can be approximated as homogeneous\nand isotropic. An FLRW metric then describes spatial expansion through a\nscale factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDifferent pressure–energy relations generate different expansion\nhistories. This is a cosmological approximation, not a claim that local\nbound systems simply expand with the scale factor.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Use a dimensionless scale factor normalized to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a(t_0)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nspatial flatness\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand initially\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Lambda=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Hubble rate is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=\\dot a/a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn terms of <strong>energy density<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand pressure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Friedmann and continuity equations are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H^2=8\\pi G\\varepsilon/(3c^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>ε<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot\\varepsilon+3H(\\varepsilon+p)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor constant equation of state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=w\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nintegration gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon\\propto a^{-3(1+w)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPressureless matter\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_m\\propto a^{-3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile radiation\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_r\\propto a^{-4}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA single component in the spatially flat model gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\propto t^{2/[3(1+w)]}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w&gt;-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>a<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H^2=\\left(\\frac{\\dot a}{a}\\right)^2=\\frac{8\\pi G}{3c^2}\\varepsilon,\\qquad\\varepsilon\\propto a^{-3(1+w)}\\quad(k=0,\\ \\Lambda=0).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>When the scale factor grows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nmatter energy density falls to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile radiation energy density falls to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a matter-only model with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\propto t^{2/3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neight times the cosmic time gives four times the scale factor. The plot\nshows these two idealized power laws normalized to the same initial\nvalues.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_101_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two normalized curves of cosmic scale factor versus time: matter-dominated and radiation-dominated power laws.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two normalized curves of cosmic scale\nfactor versus time: matter-dominated and radiation-dominated power\nlaws.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>In spatially flat single-component models, the scale factor grows\nas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t^{2/3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor matter and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t^{1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor radiation.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntriples?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What power of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes the flat radiation-dominated model?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which additional component can strongly affect realistic expansion\neven when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It falls to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>27<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/27<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof its initial value.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\propto t^{1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A cosmological constant or dark energy, set to zero in the\nexample.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Homogeneity, isotropy, individual perfect fluids, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=\\Lambda=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare model assumptions, not universal facts. The displayed Friedmann\nequation uses energy density; if mass-equivalent density is used, the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c^{-2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfactor is absent. No observational parameters or multi-component effects\nare fitted here.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Einstein dynamics relates energy density to expansion in an FLRW\nuniverse. Perturbations, horizons, and dark energy require further\nassumptions and data. The next module develops relativistic field\ntheories.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/cosmology/cosmohtml/S1\">Tong – The Expanding Universe<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §§1.2–1.3: FLRW, continuity, and Friedmann models; Subject relevance to lesson: M29-L7 – FLRW cosmology and Friedmann dynamics · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
